@page "/guides/rtl"
@using BlazorBlueprint.Icons.Lucide.Components

<PageTitle>Right-to-Left - Blazor Blueprint</PageTitle>

<div class="max-w-4xl space-y-8">
    <div class="space-y-3">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">Right-to-Left</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground">
            Wrap the layout in <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-lg">BbDirectionProvider</code>
            and the library mirrors. Use the toggle in the header to see any page in both directions.
        </p>
    </div>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Setup</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The provider goes around the whole layout, including the overlay hosts. An overlay
            renders through <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">BbPortalHost</code>,
            so it needs the direction too.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Source="Guides/Rtl/setup.txt" />
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            The provider renders no box of its own, so adding it changes no layout. It writes a
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">dir</code> attribute and cascades a
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">DirectionContext</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">How it works</h2>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Layout mirrors through CSS.</strong> Every spacing,
                border, radius and alignment utility the library ships is logical
                (<code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">margin-inline-start</code>, not
                <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">margin-left</code>), so the
                <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">dir</code> attribute is all it takes.
                A convention test keeps it that way.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Overlays follow their trigger.</strong> A popover,
                menu or dialog copies the direction from the element that opened it as it opens, the
                same way it copies a local theme. So the provider works even when the portal host
                sits outside it.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Positioning is already logical.</strong>
                <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">PopoverAlign.Start</code> and
                <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">End</code> resolve against the
                writing direction, so an end-aligned menu stays on the trailing edge.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Arrow keys follow the reading direction.</strong> In
                tabs, toggle groups, radio groups, menus and the tree,
                <BbKbd>&#8594;</BbKbd> moves the way the items are drawn rather than the way the key
                points. <BbKbd>&#8593;</BbKbd> and <BbKbd>&#8595;</BbKbd> never flip.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">No provider, no change.</strong> Without one, a
                component follows <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">CultureInfo.CurrentCulture</code>,
                which is what the library did before the provider existed.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Physical sides stay physical</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            A parameter that names a physical side keeps its promise. These do not mirror, by design
            — pass the other value if you want the other side:
        </p>
        <ul class="ms-4 list-disc space-y-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">SheetSide</code>, <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">DrawerDirection</code> and <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">SidebarSide</code></li>
            <li><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">ToastPosition</code> and <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">BadgeDotPosition</code></li>
            <li><code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">PopoverSide</code>, which reports the side an overlay actually landed on</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Not mirrored yet</h2>
        <div class="rounded-r-lg border-s-4 border-primary bg-primary/5 p-4">
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
                A few components place their content with pixel or percentage maths measured from
                the left edge, so mirroring the paint alone would put them out of step with the
                maths behind it. These stay left-to-right inside a right-to-left page:
                <strong class="text-foreground">Slider</strong>,
                <strong class="text-foreground">RangeSlider</strong>,
                <strong class="text-foreground">Carousel</strong>,
                <strong class="text-foreground">Scheduler</strong>,
                <strong class="text-foreground">EventCalendar</strong> day bands,
                <strong class="text-foreground">DashboardGrid</strong>,
                <strong class="text-foreground">Dock</strong> and
                <strong class="text-foreground">Resizable</strong>. Everything else mirrors.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Reading the direction yourself</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            A component of your own can read the same value. Resolve it through
            <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">DirectionContext.Resolve</code>, which
            falls back to the culture when there is no provider, so you never test for null.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Source="Guides/Rtl/reading.txt" />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">API Reference</h2>

        <ApiReference Since="v4" ComponentName="BbDirectionProvider">
            <ApiParameter Name="Direction" Type="TextDirection" Default="Auto">Auto follows the current culture. LeftToRight and RightToLeft set it explicitly.</ApiParameter>
            <ApiParameter Name="ChildContent" Type="RenderFragment?">The content the direction applies to.</ApiParameter>
        </ApiReference>

        <ApiReference Since="v4" ComponentName="DirectionContext">
            <ApiParameter Name="Direction" Type="TextDirection">The direction as configured, before Auto is resolved.</ApiParameter>
            <ApiParameter Name="IsRightToLeft" Type="bool">The resolved direction. Auto resolves against the current culture.</ApiParameter>
            <ApiParameter Name="Attribute" Type="string">The value for an HTML <code>dir</code> attribute: <code>"rtl"</code> or <code>"ltr"</code>.</ApiParameter>
            <ApiParameter Name="Resolve(DirectionContext?)" Type="static bool">Resolves a cascaded context, falling back to the current culture when there is no provider.</ApiParameter>
        </ApiReference>
    </section>
</div>
